    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d379">

                <pb facs="0587" n="541" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v02/pg541"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.00/15629½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Cornish, N. H.</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1915-07-29">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">29 July, 1915</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: The many distressing and
                    disturbing despatches pouring in from Mexico perplex me sadly as to what our
                    immediate duty is. I note the messages you are sending to Villa and indirectly
                    to Zapata, and I suppose that they represent all that we can do for the present.
                    If <hi rendition="#underline">any</hi>thing else should occur to you as
                    practicable, will you not let me have the suggestion to think over. I feel that
                    a final crisis at Mexico City might make more deliberate plans at any moment
                    next to impossible.</p>
                <p>Faithfully Yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. W.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
